course: dictionary
lesson: 3653
title: Firmly: strong or serious
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Hold the handrail firmly.» Firmly can mean strongly and steadily, so that something does not move or come loose. Your hand should stay tight on the rail, so you do not slip.
explain: «No, not one little song,» replied the Rat firmly, though his heart bled. Here firmly means in a clear, serious way. The Rat is not shouting, but he is calm and will not change his mind.
explain: Check the meaning by putting it back into the sentence. If «he said it in a clear, serious way» still makes sense, firmly is the right meaning. If you are holding something, ask whether it is strong and steady.
fact: Firmly is an adverb, so it tells us how something is done.
fact: Firmly can mean strongly and steadily, so that something does not move: «hold the handrail firmly».
fact: Firmly can mean in a clear, serious way that shows you will not change your mind: «said firmly».
fact: In first aid, an adult may press firmly on a cut with a clean cloth.
fact: Firmly often goes with words like «hold», «believe», «said» and «closed».
exercise: «Hold the handrail firmly as you climb the ladder.» What does firmly mean here? | Quickly, like a race | *Strongly, so the rail does not move | Loudly, so everyone can hear | Gently, with a soft touch
exercise: «No, not one little song,» replied the Rat firmly. What does firmly show about the Rat? | He is laughing and joking | He is tired and sleepy | *He is clear and serious and will not change his mind | He is scared and running away
exercise: Which word could replace firmly in «press firmly on the cut»? | Sadly | Happily | *Hard and steady | Quietly
test: «Toad is held firmly by the shoulder.» Does this mean Toad can wriggle free? | Yes, he is held loosely so he can wriggle free | Yes, he is held sadly | *No, he is held strongly so he cannot slip away | No, he is not being held at all
test: «Say No firmly to things that are not okay.» What does firmly suggest? | You are playing a game | *You mean it clearly and will not change your mind | You are asking a question | You are whispering it
exam: Which meaning fits «firmly closed» for a door? | Open wide | *Shut tight so it does not open | Painted blue | Very quickly opened
